<div class="takeaways">
  <p class="takeaways__title">Storm-damage roof coverage: the short version</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Most Texas homeowners policies cover sudden roof damage from hail and wind; they do not cover wear, age or poor maintenance.</li>
    <li>Wind and hail deductibles in Texas are often a percentage of your dwelling coverage — commonly 1% to 2%, sometimes more — not a flat dollar amount.</li>
    <li>Replacement cost (RCV) policies pay to replace the roof; actual cash value (ACV) policies subtract depreciation, which can be large on an older roof.</li>
    <li>Many policies now exclude “cosmetic” damage, especially on metal roofs. Read your declarations page before a storm, not after.</li>
    <li>Report damage promptly, prevent further damage, and document everything with dated photos.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<p class="lead">Storm season in Central Texas brings hail, straight-line winds and sudden downpours, and the first question after a bad storm is always whether insurance will pay. This guide explains roof storm damage coverage in plain terms: what your policy usually covers, how wind damage roof coverage and hail deductibles work in Texas, and how an insurance claim for roof hail damage in Austin typically unfolds.</p>

<p>Every policy is different, so treat this as a general overview, not legal or insurance advice. Your declarations page, your policy wording and your agent are the final word on your coverage. What follows is what we see again and again when Austin Roofing inspects storm-damaged roofs and meets with adjusters across Travis, Williamson and Hays County.</p>

<h2 id="h-why-roof-damage-is-a-major-insurance-issue">Why Roof Damage Is a Major Insurance Issue in Texas</h2>
<p>Texas consistently ranks among the states with the most hail claims in the country, and the Austin–Round Rock corridor sits in an active part of that hail belt. A single spring storm can damage thousands of roofs in a few minutes. Wind adds to the problem: thunderstorm outflow and straight-line winds can exceed 60–70 mph, lifting shingles and breaking seal strips.</p>
<p>A damaged roof also rarely stays a roof problem. Once water gets in, it reaches insulation, drywall, wiring and flooring. That is why insurers scrutinize roof claims closely, and why roof claims are a major driver of rising homeowners premiums statewide. Understanding your coverage before a storm helps you avoid surprises when you need it most.</p>

<h3 id="h-when-hail-season-hits-austin">When Hail and Wind Season Hits Austin</h3>
<p>Central Texas hail activity peaks in spring, roughly March through June, when warm Gulf moisture collides with cold fronts and drylines moving in from the west. Fall storms can also produce hail, and summer thunderstorms bring damaging outflow winds. Hail around 1 inch in diameter (quarter size) is often cited as the point where asphalt shingles start to suffer functional damage, though older, sun-brittled shingles can be damaged by smaller stones and wind-driven hail hits harder than hail falling straight down. Because damage is not always visible from the ground, many homeowners do not realize their roof was hit until a leak shows up months later — which is exactly when a claim becomes harder to prove. If a storm with notable hail passes over your neighborhood, an inspection within a few weeks is cheap insurance.</p>

<h2 id="h-types-of-storm-damage-that-are-commonly-covered">Types of Storm Damage That Are Commonly Covered</h2>
<p>Most homeowners policies (such as the HO-3 or Texas HO-A/HO-B style forms) cover sudden, accidental physical damage. For roofs, that usually includes:</p>
<div class="feature-grid">
  <div class="feature"><h3>Hail damage</h3><p>Fractured or bruised shingles, granule loss that exposes the mat, dented metal panels, vents, gutters and flashing. Our <a href="/services/hail-damage-repair/">hail damage repair</a> page shows what we document.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Wind damage</h3><p>Missing, creased or lifted shingles, broken seal strips, peeled flashing and blown-off ridge cap — see <a href="/services/wind-damage-repair/">wind damage roof repair</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Falling trees and limbs</h3><p>Impact damage from trees or large branches knocked down by a storm.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Resulting interior water damage</h3><p>Water that enters through a storm-created opening is often covered, as long as you take reasonable steps to prevent further damage.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The key word is <em>sudden</em>. A roof that was punctured by hail in April is a storm claim. A roof that has been slowly losing granules for a decade is not.</p>

<h2 id="h-what-roof-damage-is-often-not-covered">What Roof Damage Is Often Not Covered</h2>
<p>Denials usually come down to one of these exclusions or limitations:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Wear and tear, age and deterioration</strong> — curling, cracking and granule loss from years of Texas sun.</li>
  <li><strong>Lack of maintenance</strong> — long-term leaks, rotted decking, clogged valleys or failed sealant that was never addressed.</li>
  <li><strong>Faulty installation or defective materials</strong> — these are workmanship or manufacturer warranty issues, not insurance issues. Our <a href="/blog/residential-roofing-warranty-coverage-guide-for-homeowners/">roofing warranty guide</a> explains the difference.</li>
  <li><strong>Cosmetic damage exclusions</strong> — many Texas policies now include endorsements that exclude damage affecting only appearance, such as dents in metal roofing that do not cause leaks.</li>
  <li><strong>Damage not reported in time</strong> — waiting too long can make it hard to prove when the damage happened.</li>
  <li><strong>Mold</strong> — often excluded or capped unless it results from a covered loss and is addressed promptly.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><p><strong>Cosmetic damage matters in Austin.</strong> If you have a metal or stone-coated steel roof, check whether your policy has a cosmetic damage exclusion. Hail that dents panels without breaking the finish or causing leaks may not be paid under that endorsement.</p></div>

<h2 id="h-actual-cash-value-vs-replacement-cost-coverage">Actual Cash Value vs Replacement Cost Coverage</h2>
<p>This single policy detail often decides how much money you actually receive.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th></th><th>Replacement Cost Value (RCV)</th><th>Actual Cash Value (ACV)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>What it pays</td><td>Cost to replace with like kind and quality, minus deductible</td><td>Replacement cost minus depreciation, minus deductible</td></tr>
    <tr><td>How payment usually works</td><td>An initial ACV payment, then the withheld depreciation once work is completed and invoiced</td><td>A single payment for the depreciated value</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Effect of roof age</td><td>Smaller, as long as you complete the work</td><td>Large — an older roof can be depreciated heavily</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Premium</td><td>Higher</td><td>Lower</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Example: a roof that costs $16,000 to replace, with a $4,000 deductible and $6,000 in depreciation. Under RCV, you would typically receive $6,000 first and another $6,000 after the work is done (total $12,000). Under ACV, you would typically receive $6,000 total. Numbers are illustrative only.</p>
<p>Some policies use a hybrid — RCV for most of the house but ACV for roofs over a certain age, or a roof payment schedule that pays a percentage based on age and material. Check your declarations page for language like “roof surface payment schedule” or “ACV roof endorsement.”</p>

<h2 id="h-deductibles-and-storm-damage-claims">Deductibles and Storm Damage Claims</h2>
<p>In much of Texas, wind and hail deductibles are written as a <strong>percentage of your dwelling coverage (Coverage A)</strong>, not a flat amount. Common figures are 1% or 2%, though some policies carry higher percentages.</p>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>A home insured for $400,000 with a 1% wind/hail deductible has a $4,000 deductible.</li>
  <li>The same home with a 2% deductible pays the first $8,000.</li>
  <li>If the damage costs less than the deductible, a claim pays nothing but may still appear in your claims history.</li>
</ul>
<p>Texas law prohibits contractors from waiving, absorbing or rebating your deductible, so be cautious of anyone offering to “cover” it. A legitimate contractor will help you understand the damage and the scope, but the deductible is your responsibility.</p>

<h2 id="h-the-roof-insurance-claim-process">The Roof Insurance Claim Process</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><h3>Make sure everyone is safe and stop further damage</h3><p>Tarp openings or call for <a href="/services/emergency-roof-repair/">emergency roof repair</a>. Policies generally require you to take reasonable steps to prevent further damage, and the cost of temporary repairs is often reimbursable. Keep receipts.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Document the damage</h3><p>Take dated photos of the roof (from the ground), gutters, siding, windows, cars, patio furniture and interior stains. Note the storm date. Our <a href="/roofing-tools/hail-damage-checker/">hail size and damage checker</a> can help you gauge what hail of a given size typically does.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Get a professional roof inspection</h3><p>A <a href="/services/roof-inspection/">roof inspection with a photo report</a> shows whether the damage is storm-related, how widespread it is, and whether it is worth filing.</p></li>
  <li><h3>File the claim promptly</h3><p>Call your insurer or file online. Many policies require notice “promptly” and some set deadlines, often within a year of the storm. You will receive a claim number and an adjuster assignment.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Adjuster inspection</h3><p>The insurer’s adjuster inspects the roof and prepares an estimate. Austin Roofing can be on site to show the damage we documented and make sure nothing is missed.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Review the estimate and payment</h3><p>Compare the insurer’s scope with your contractor’s. Missing items — drip edge, starter, ridge, code-required upgrades, decking — are common. You or your representative can ask the insurer to reconsider with supporting documentation.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Repair or replace, then collect recoverable depreciation</h3><p>Under an RCV policy, submit the final invoice and completion photos to collect withheld depreciation.</p></li>
</ol>
<p>Texas law sets time limits for insurers to acknowledge, investigate and pay claims, and the Texas Department of Insurance publishes consumer guides that explain them. If you ever feel a claim is being handled unfairly, TDI is the place to start.</p>
<p>Learn more about how we support homeowners through this process on our <a href="/services/roof-insurance-claims/">roof insurance claim help</a> page. Note that Austin Roofing is a roofing contractor, not a public adjuster: we document damage and meet your adjuster, but we do not negotiate claims on your behalf.</p>

<h2 id="h-why-some-roof-claims-are-underpaid">Why Some Roof Claims Are Underpaid</h2>
<p>Most adjusters are trying to get it right, but underpayments happen. Common reasons include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Incomplete scope</strong> — only one slope approved when damage is on several, or accessories left off.</li>
  <li><strong>Missed damage</strong> — hail bruising is hard to see without hands-on testing; soft metals and vents tell the story.</li>
  <li><strong>Repair instead of replacement</strong> — spot repairs approved where matching shingles are no longer available.</li>
  <li><strong>Pricing gaps</strong> — estimate software that lags behind post-storm material and labor costs.</li>
  <li><strong>Code upgrades excluded</strong> — items required by current code that are only covered if you have ordinance or law coverage.</li>
</ul>
<p>Good documentation is your best tool. Ask for the adjuster’s written estimate, compare it line by line with your contractor’s, and request a re-inspection if damage was missed.</p>

<h2 id="h-roof-age-codes-and-coverage-limits">Roof Age, Codes and Coverage Limits</h2>
<p>Roof age affects coverage more than many homeowners realize. Insurers may apply heavier depreciation, require a roof inspection at renewal, or move older roofs to ACV coverage. Some carriers will not write new policies on roofs past a certain age.</p>
<p>Building codes add another layer. When a roof is replaced, current code may require items the old roof never had — specific underlayment, drip edge or ventilation. <strong>Ordinance or law coverage</strong> pays for code-required upgrades; without it, those costs may fall on you. It is worth asking your agent whether your policy includes it and at what percentage.</p>

<h2 id="h-avoiding-common-post-storm-mistakes">Avoiding Common Post-Storm Mistakes</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Do not climb onto a wet or damaged roof yourself — inspect from the ground or with binoculars.</li>
  <li>Do not sign a contract with a door-knocker on the spot; check that the company is local and can be reached after the storm crews leave.</li>
  <li>Do not assign your insurance benefits to a contractor without understanding what you are signing.</li>
  <li>Do not throw away damaged items before the adjuster sees them or you have photographed them.</li>
  <li>Do not wait months to report — damage gets harder to tie to a specific storm.</li>
  <li>Do keep all receipts, photos, emails and claim notes in one place.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="h-final-thoughts">Final Thoughts on Roof Storm Damage Coverage</h2>
<p>Roof damage insurance claims in Austin go smoothest when homeowners know their deductible, understand whether they have ACV or RCV coverage, and act quickly with good documentation. If a storm has passed over your neighborhood, start with a free inspection. Austin Roofing will tell you honestly whether the damage looks like it meets your deductible, document it with photos, and meet your adjuster if you file. <a href="/contact-us/">Contact us</a> or call any time — we offer 24/7 emergency service.</p>
